llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
144; needs the base image's gfx803 SGEMM shim, since stock rocBLAS fp32
GEMMs are wrong on gfx803): prompt attention at head size 512 as two fp32
GEMMs with an exact softmax.
gfx803-specific: engages only for cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4.

WHY
---
Prompt batches at head size 512 (Gemma 4's five full-attention layers, 16
query heads over 2 K/V heads) run on the flash-attention tile kernel. At
head size 512 its 32-column tile holds too much per lane: at the 128 VGPRs
its 512 threads leave it, it spills about 6 KB per lane to scratch, and a
256-thread configuration with 256 VGPRs still spills 1433 VGPRs. It runs at
0.16 TFLOPS. Patch 131's layout does not stretch that far either: a 16-key
K/V tile pair in fp32 would take 66 KB of shared memory. A 512-token batch,
one layer (test-backend-ops perf, q8_0 K/V):

    K/V length        2048     8192    16384
    tile kernel        214      851     1711 ms

so Gemma 4 26B-A4B reads a 19,500-token prompt at 74 tok/s, against 220 tok/s
for a 2,000-token one.

WHAT
----
fattn-gcn4-gemm.cuh adds ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_gemm(). For each K/V
head it converts K and V to fp32 and, for chunks of query tokens, runs

    S = scale * K^T Q    one strided-batched SGEMM over the K/V head's query
                         heads, with the K/V head broadcast (stride 0)
    P = softmax(S + mask) per column, in place; a fully masked column is zero
    O = V P              a second strided-batched SGEMM that writes straight
                         into dst, whose rows interleave the query heads

The SGEMMs go to the base image's gfx803 SGEMM shim. The chunk keeps S at
32 MiB. All scratch (the fp32 K and V of one head and S) lies in dst's extra
space, which ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_get_alloc_size() reports, so the
compute buffer reserves it as it does the tile kernel's fp16 K/V: Gemma 4 at
a 32k context 1036 -> 1068 MiB.

ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext() takes this path in the tile branch, after 144's
decode kernel, for gfx803, head size 512, two or more query tokens, f32 Q, an
f16 mask or none, no sinks, no ALiBi and no logit softcap, and K/V types with
an fp32 converter. Every other case keeps its kernel.

Result on an RX 590, 512-token batch, one layer (q8_0 K/V, GQA 8):

    K/V length        2048     8192    16384
    tile kernel        214      851     1711 ms
    this path         17.6     63.0      132 ms  (2.0-2.2 TFLOPS)

Of the 8192-key batch, the score GEMM takes 46% (2.3 TFLOPS), V*P 37% (3.0
TFLOPS), the softmax 16%.

Gemma 4 26B-A4B-it heretic-ara i1-Q4_K_M, experts in system RAM,
llama-server, q8_0 KV cache, prompt tok/s:

    eight ~2,000-token prompts   220 -> 305
    7,800-token prompt           136 -> 298
    19,500-token prompt           74 -> 265

Generation is unchanged (one query token takes 144's kernel).

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590), in the image built with this patch:
- test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT: all 622 q8_0 cases pass.
- test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT -p hsk=512: 178/178
  pass against the CPU backend, among them 49 multi-token cases on this
  path. 144 added cases cover 2 K/V heads, GQA 1, 2 and 8, KV length
  113-4096, 2, 33 and 512 query tokens, q8_0 and f16 K/V, with and without
  a mask; head size 512 supports neither GQA 1 nor a missing mask, so the
  backend reports those as not supported.
- llama-perplexity KL divergence against the CPU backend, prompt batches of
  512: 2 chunks of 2048 tokens (positions past the 1024-token sliding window)
  0.304 with the tile kernel, 0.300 with this path; 8 chunks of 512 tokens
  0.414 / 0.416. Two CPU code paths (with and without weight repacking)
  differ by 0.36 on this model.
- Gemma 4 through the image: prompts 297 tok/s at 7,800 tokens and 265 at
  19,500; generation 21.6, 21.1 and 20.1 tok/s at ~2,000, 7,800 and 19,500.
- Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (head size 256, not on this path), eight ~2,000-token
  prompts, 300 greedy tokens against the image before 144: the same text
  for all eight, generation 40.21 / 40.13 tok/s, prompts 314-320 tok/s in
  both. No GPU fault.
